const takeaway = `
<p>Sorting works because it destroys the only information the question does not care
about — the order — and leaves two strings that are equal exactly when the letter counts
match. It is three lines and hard to get wrong, and it costs <code>O(n log n)</code> to
rebuild something you could have counted directly.</p>

<p>The tally asks a different question. For each letter of <code>t</code> it asks: <em>does
the table still owe me one of these?</em> A lookup answers that in constant time, so the
whole job is two linear passes. The table never holds more than 26 rows, which is why the
space is <code>O(1)</code> rather than <code>O(n)</code>.</p>

<p>That is the same move as <strong>Two Sum</strong>'s hash map and <strong>Majority
Element</strong>'s counter: instead of comparing everything to everything, ask one question
per element that a lookup can answer, and let a table carry the memory. Once you see it
here, most of the "count the things" problems are the same problem.</p>

<p>Two details the walkthrough makes visible. The length check is not an optimisation, it is
what makes the rule sound: because the totals are equal, "no row went negative" is enough to
conclude that no row was left positive either. And the early <code>false</code> on a negative
row is safe — a row that has gone below zero can never climb back, since <code>t</code> only
subtracts.</p>

<p>The Unicode follow-up is where the two approaches separate again. A fixed
<code>[26]int</code> stops working the moment a character is not <code>a</code>–<code>z</code>;
swap it for a hash map keyed by code point and the algorithm is unchanged, with space now
proportional to the number of distinct characters. Sorting adapts without any edit, which is
its one real advantage. Grapheme clusters — an emoji with a skin-tone modifier, an accent
written as a combining mark — are a separate problem: normalise first, then count, or you will
call two identical-looking strings different.</p>`;
